; link/pop_engine.link — the ENGINE memory map (P3.5).
;
; Separate from link/pop.link because the engine and the P1.x probes have
; incompatible constraints. The probes are pinned at $0200 by a harness contract
; (it reads probe_status at $0203 and gates the LOADM on the bytes at $0200); the
; engine cannot live there at all, and the reason is measured, not stylistic.
;
; WHY THE ENGINE IS NOT AT $0200 (P3.5, the whole of it):
;   Color BASIC's line-input buffer is at $02DC. LOADM places the program, and
;   then the operator types EXEC to start it -- and DECB stores those keystrokes
;   IN THE BUFFER, on top of the program that was just loaded. Caught on the bus:
;
;     f708  $02DD <- $45   'E'      from DECB, PC=$A3E7
;     f717  $02DE <- $58   'X'
;     f726  $02DF <- $45   'E'
;     f735  $02E0 <- $43   'C'
;
;   $02DD was `sta probe_phase` and $02E0 was `ldx seq_beat`. The command that
;   starts the program corrupts the program.
;
;   It is vicious because it is LAYOUT-SENSITIVE: whether it matters depends on
;   which instructions happen to sit under $02DC, so adding four bytes of
;   diagnostic changes the symptom or hides it. And it never appears on the poked
;   path (P3.2/P3.3, and the live viewer) because nothing types EXEC there --
;   which is exactly why the sequence ran correctly for Jay and "failed" under the
;   test.
;
; THE THREE REGIONS A LOADM+EXEC PROGRAM MUST CLEAR:
;   $02DC-$03D5   Color BASIC line-input buffer  <- typing EXEC lands here
;   $0400-$05FF   the text screen                <- DECB prints "OK" into it
;   $0600-$09FF   DBUF0/DBUF1/FAT/FCBs           <- LOADM itself uses these
;                 [karateka docs/project/decb-loadm-boot-gates.md; POP idioms §23]
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   $0A00-$1BFF   intro asset bundle (read from disk at run time)
;   $1C00-$1EFF   caption save buffer
;   $1F00-$1F06   disk primitive parameters (-DDR_VARBASE=0x1F00)
;   $2000-...     ENGINE (section `prog`)  <- load + exec address
;   $7800-$78FF   trace ring buffer
;   $7900-$7CA6   KERNEL (section `code`)
;   $7F00         stack top
;   $8000-$F7FF   MMU draw window
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; $2000 sits above every DECB region above and below the graphics pages' end at
; $25FF, which DECB does not touch unless a BASIC graphics command runs. The
; engine's own runtime data stays where P3.4 put it -- also chosen to be clear of
; DECB, for the same reason, one dispatch earlier.

section prog load 2000
section code load 7900

entry probe_entry
